// src/dig_query.pdx -- pdxdig M2-001 real UDP query wire scaffold
// (pdxdig#4). BLOCKED on R100-PREP-002 / design/networking/
// r100-user-tools-plan.md §12.1 (kernel-side SOCK_DGRAM extension).
//
// Wave: R100  Milestone: M2-001 (pdxdig#4)
// Doc:  design/networking/r100-user-tools-plan.md §6 + §12.1 + §13.5
//
// =============================================================================
// THE BLOCKER (documented per issue instructions)
// =============================================================================
//
// pdxdig's real DNS query path needs a UDP round-trip: send a query
// packet to the resolver, wait for a response, hand the response to
// DnsWalker (src/dns_walker.pdx). That round-trip is `libpdx-net`'s
// job -- the R100 wave's shared resolver library other satellites
// (pdxcurl, pdxping) also consume. R100-PREP-002 tracks the kernel-
// side SOCK_DGRAM extension libpdx-net's implementation needs
// (sendto/recvfrom over a UDP socket kind); until that PREP item
// lands, libpdx-net cannot expose a real `net_resolve`, and pdxdig
// cannot link against it.
//
// =============================================================================
// THE REAL EXTERN CONTRACT (documented now so the swap-to-real
// landing is a body-only edit, not a signature change)
// =============================================================================
//
//   extern net_resolve(host_ptr, host_len, qtype, out_addr) -> u64
//     rdi = host_ptr   -- query name bytes (NOT required NUL-terminated;
//                          host_len is authoritative)
//     rsi = host_len    -- query name byte count
//     rdx = qtype       -- RR type (1 = A at pdxdig v1.x; libpdx-net's
//                          resolver parses no other RR type per this
//                          repo's README)
//     rcx = out_addr    -- pointer to a 4-byte out-param the callee
//                          fills with the resolved IPv4 answer (LE
//                          byte order, matching DnsQueryRecord@0.1's
//                          own `answer_ip:u32` field in src/main.pdx)
//     rax = 0 on success, -errno on failure (NXDOMAIN/TIMEOUT/
//           MALFORMED_RESPONSE map to distinct negative values per
//           libpdx-net's own contract once it lands -- not yet
//           finalised at this scaffold landing)
//
// =============================================================================
// WHY THIS FILE DOES NOT EMIT A REAL `call net_resolve`
// =============================================================================
//
// `tools/build.sh` links every `src/*.o` in this repo into ONE flat
// ELF via `ld -T link.ld --fatal-warnings` (no partial-link, no
// per-milestone object subset) -- there is no cross-repo link step
// that would let an unresolved `net_resolve` UND symbol sit dormant
// until libpdx-net is wired. Emitting a bare `call net_resolve` here
// today would break EVERY build of this repo, not just the M2-001
// call site, until libpdx-net lands in `manifest.pdxproj`'s `deps:`
// list. `paideia-os/mv`'s `ElevateGate` (src/elevate_gate.pdx) and
// `paideia-os/mkfs.pdxfs`'s own LE-001 landing hit this exact
// constraint first and resolved it the same way this file does: the
// real extern signature is documented verbatim (above), the call
// site's future shape is documented (below), and the callable
// surface pdxdig actually links today is a WEAK STUB.
//
// =============================================================================
// dig_query_resolve -- the WEAK STUB
// =============================================================================
//
// `dig_query_resolve(host_ptr, host_len, qtype, out_addr) -> u64` is
// the SAME 4-argument shape as the real `net_resolve` extern above --
// this is intentional: the day libpdx-net lands in `deps:`, this
// function's `block:` becomes a single `call net_resolve; ret;` and
// every caller (main.pdx's future M2-001 dispatch arm) needs zero
// changes.
//
// Until then, the stub ignores every argument (fixed behaviour:
// there is no real resolver to query) and:
//   * writes the 4-byte sentinel 0xDEADBEEF (LE: EF BE AD DE) into
//     `[out_addr]`, UNLESS out_addr == 0 (a not-yet-wired caller
//     passing a null out-param cannot fault this leaf);
//   * returns 0xDEADBEEF in rax -- deliberately NOT a small status
//     code (0 / a small negative errno), so a caller can distinguish
//     "the stub answered" from "the real net_resolve answered"
//     without a separate flag. 0xDEADBEEF is also never a routable
//     unicast IPv4 answer, so it reads unambiguously as a placeholder
//     wherever it surfaces (a packet trace, a log line, a
//     DnsQueryRecord@0.1 `answer_ip` field).
//
// Call site status: NOT YET WIRED into Main::_start at this landing
// (pdxdig#4 is scoped as "scaffold the wire", not "wire it up") --
// the no-flag argv path still exits 3 with `pdxdig: udp stub\n` per
// v1.2.0 (src/main.pdx). A follow-up M2-001-b landing threads
// `dig_query_resolve`'s return + out-param into the record marshal
// and terminal dispatch once the fixed-sentinel-vs-real-answer
// distinction actually needs to gate behaviour.
//
// Encoder discipline (paideia-as 0.36+ pitfalls, per user memory)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
//   - No `test rN, rN` (every zero-check is `cmp rN, 0`).
//   - No 2-op `imul r, imm` (unused).
//   - No `and rN, imm64` on r8..r15 (unused).
//   - No `neg` mnemonic (unused).
//   - Byte stores via `mov rax, imm; mov_b [ptr + N], rax`.
//   - Module basename PascalCase (`DigQuery` for `dig_query.pdx`).
//   - <= 4 curried args (`dig_query_resolve` takes exactly 4).
//   - Every label prefixed `dq_`.

module DigQuery = structure {

  // Sentinel documentation constant -- not memory-loaded (the body
  // hardcodes the four LE bytes + the movabs literal directly),
  // matching the "constants as human-readable labels" convention
  // AuditFileSink::AFS_OK and friends establish.
  pub let DQ_SENTINEL_ADDR : u64 = 0x00000000DEADBEEF

  // -------------------------------------------------------------------
  // dig_query_resolve(host_ptr, host_len, qtype, out_addr) -> u64
  //   rdi = host_ptr, rsi = host_len, rdx = qtype, rcx = out_addr
  //   rax = 0x00000000DEADBEEF (WEAK STUB sentinel; see file header)
  //
  // Pure leaf, effects: {mem} for the conditional out-param write.
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------
  pub let dig_query_resolve : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64
    !{mem} @{} =
    fn (host_ptr: u64) (host_len: u64) (qtype: u64) (out_addr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem},
      capabilities: {},
      justification: "pdxdig.M2-001 (pdxdig#4) DigQuery::dig_query_resolve WEAK STUB. Real body (post libpdx-net dep landing) is `call net_resolve; ret` with the identical 4-arg ABI documented in this file's header (rdi=host_ptr, rsi=host_len, rdx=qtype, rcx=out_addr). Until libpdx-net lands in manifest.pdxproj deps: and R100-PREP-002/§12.1 unblocks a real UDP round-trip, this leaf ignores host_ptr/host_len/qtype entirely (fixed behaviour -- no resolver exists to query), and: (1) if out_addr != 0, writes the 4-byte LE sentinel 0xDEADBEEF (bytes 0xEF,0xBE,0xAD,0xDE at out_addr[0..3]) via four `mov rax, imm; mov_b [rcx+N], rax` stores -- guarded by `cmp rcx, 0; je dq_resolve_no_out` so a null out-param cannot fault this leaf; (2) always returns the full 64-bit sentinel 0x00000000DEADBEEF in rax via `mov rax, 0x00000000DEADBEEF` (explicit 16-hex-digit movabs form, matching src/main.pdx's own magic-constant convention, so no ambiguity between a sign-extended imm32 encoding and a genuine 64-bit zero-extended load). The sentinel is deliberately NOT status-code-shaped (0 / small negative errno) so a caller can tell 'stub answered' from 'real net_resolve answered' without a separate flag, and 0xDEADBEEF is never a routable unicast IPv4 answer so it reads unambiguously as a placeholder in any downstream trace. NOT YET CALLED from Main::_start at this landing (pdxdig#4 scope is 'scaffold the wire' -- see file header 'Call site status'). Pure leaf: no callee-save touch, no push/pop, no stack use. Encoder discipline (per user memory `pdx encoder pitfalls`): no `test` (cmp rcx, 0 for the null-guard); no 2-op `imul` (unused); no `and` on r8..r15 (unused); no `neg` (unused); exactly 4 curried args (at the documented ceiling, not over it); module basename DigQuery matches file stem dig_query.pdx; labels prefixed dq_.",
      block: {
        cmp rcx, 0;
        je dq_resolve_no_out;

        mov rax, 239;                             // 0xEF (byte 0, LE)
        mov_b [rcx + 0], rax;
        mov rax, 190;                             // 0xBE (byte 1)
        mov_b [rcx + 1], rax;
        mov rax, 173;                             // 0xAD (byte 2)
        mov_b [rcx + 2], rax;
        mov rax, 222;                             // 0xDE (byte 3)
        mov_b [rcx + 3], rax;

      dq_resolve_no_out:
        mov rax, 0x00000000DEADBEEF;
        ret;
      }
    }

}
